  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 306 AM EDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nThe CWA remains fairly quiet as drier air under northerly flow \nsubsides over the UP. This is supported by sprawling weak high \npressure ridging from Hudson Bay/northern Quebec across northern \nOntario, Lake Superior, Upper MI, WI, and MN, continuing down into \nthe Plains. The trough responsible for the heavy rainfall observed \nover parts of the UP on Friday has now made its way to IN, with an \nassociated 1005 mb sfc low beneath it. GOES WV imagery highlights a \npositively tilted ridge over the northern plains moving east toward \nthe Great Lakes. \n\nThe rest of today remains dry and quiet as ridging weakens, but \nremains mostly stationary overhead. Any patchy dense fog in the far \nwest clears out for plenty of sunshine today. Temperatures will be \nslightly cooler again in the 70s with mid to upper 60s near Lake \nSuperior. With afternoon mixing, dewpoints fall into the low 50s \nallowing for much more pleasant humidity. The drier air mass, mostly \nclear skies, and weak high pressure ridging tonight allow for \nradiative cooling to yield a chilly summer night. No frost/freeze \nconcerns, but lows are expected in the 40s in the interior with \nlakeshores settling into the the upper 40s to mid 50s. While not in \nthe forecast, would not be surprised if a few RAWS sites dipped\nbriefly into the upper 30s tonight.\n\nDry weather persists into Monday under high pressure, but with \ntemperatures warming back into the upper 70s to lower 80s as the mid \nlevel ridge continues eastward over the western Great Lakes and 850 \nmb temps warm to 15-17 C. The next chance of precipitation then \ncomes on Tuesday as a trough approaches from the northwest and \ndrives another cold front through the UP. This could also bring \nanother round of thunderstorms to the area, but severe potential is \nTBD given uncertainty over timing and degree of instability and \nshear. Temperatures look to drop down into the 70s again on \nWednesday behind the front, but will quickly recover back into the \n80s on Thursday as additional systems bring more chances for \nprecipitation towards the end of the week.",
